      "writtenOn": "2026-08-27T19:22:29Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "http-body-limit defaults to 30 MB (and allows values up to 512). It is  intended for large non-JSON request bodies such as firmware uploads. Do we really want parseStringAsJson() to permit JSON payloads up to that same limit?\n\nparser limits depth/value count protect us some..",
      "revId": "f7605be5c1f683f16336bef1af3223e8dcc30826",
      "serverId": "adbd0a64-1f21-4d83-b585-671fe73cb6e4"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "af2f5cf5_8e77aba1",
        "filename": "http/parsing.hpp",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 196,
      "author": {
        "id": 1000153
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-11-19T16:08:10Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "To reiterate what Gunnar said, we set limits based on available memory.  Json parsing in its current form is a multiplier on memory usage because of the structure overhead.\n\nI\u0027m open to increasing this to a reasonable value, but a 30MB-512MB json payload is unreasonable for a BMC to ever be parsing, and could only be a DOS attack.",
      "parentUuid": "053c49fe_64b7f955",
      "revId": "f7605be5c1f683f16336bef1af3223e8dcc30826",
      "serverId": "adbd0a64-1f21-4d83-b585-671fe73cb6e4"
    }
